// Fixture: string_escaped_quote
// (Renamed from string_escaped_quote_current_behavior -- see History below.)
//
// Rule under test: GDDL Spec v4, String Literal Escaping. `\"` inside a
// string literal resolves to a single literal double-quote character;
// the backslash itself is consumed, not retained.
//
// History: this fixture originally golden-locked CONFIRMED-BUGGY current
// behavior (the resolved value retained literal backslashes, e.g.
// `He said \"hi\" to me` rather than `He said "hi" to me`), deliberately
// flagged as "not a compliance test, no rule to check against" at the
// time -- the spec said nothing about escape sequences yet, and
// `_strip_quotes()`/the equivalent field-value site never actually
// unescaped anything, just stripped the outer quote pair. That design
// gap is now resolved (GDDL_Specification_v4.md, String Literal
// Escaping) and the underlying bug fixed (parser.py's shared
// `_unescape_string_content`, used by both `_strip_quotes` for
// identifier descriptions and resolve.py's field-value handling, which
// had the identical bug independently). This fixture now golden-locks
// the CORRECT, intended behavior and is an ordinary compliance test
// like any other -- the prior golden capture under the old name and
// old (buggy) expected value should be treated as superseded, not a
// second value to reconcile against.

define Message
	text = string 40

Message QuotedGreeting
	text = "He said \"hi\" to me"

// Expected:
//   Compiles cleanly, no error (20 bytes of raw source between the
//   quotes unescapes to 18 characters/bytes of actual content, all
//   ASCII, comfortably under `string 40`'s 39-byte capacity).
//   QuotedGreeting.text == the literal 18-character sequence with the
//   backslashes CONSUMED and real quote characters in their place:
//   `He said "hi" to me`. Character-by-character:
//   ['H','e',' ','s','a','i','d',' ','"','h','i','"',' ','t','o',' ','m','e']
//   -- 18 elements, no backslash characters anywhere in the result.
